 "chosen": 0,
 "why": "The canal is a visible, physical line the audience can track in every wide shot; the rule creates instant geography (safe side / dry side). The giant beating its own rule by building a bridge is a reversal people won't see coming, and the hero's flaw (always goes back) becomes the climax.",
 "outline": [
  [
   "Hook",
   "0-4s",
   "Wide on a city running to the canal; the giant stops dead at the water's edge."
  ],
  [
   "Rule",
   "4-10s",
   "Its foot touches the water \u2014 steam, recoil. It can't cross water."
  ],
  [
   "Hero + stakes",
   "10-20s",
   "Nora made it over the bridge. Leo didn't. He's alone on the dry side."
  ],
  [
   "Flaw",
   "20-27s",
   "Chief: nobody crosses back. Narrator: Nora never leaves anything behind."
  ],
  [
   "Reversal",
   "27-38s",
   "It tears buildings down and fills the canal \u2014 building a bridge."
  ],
  [
   "Clever use",
   "38-48s",
   "Nora: open every hydrant, flood the plaza. It's trapped on its own rubble."
  ],
  [
   "Twist",
   "48-54s",
   "But Leo is on the rubble too \u2014 right next to it."
  ],
  [
   "Climax",
   "54-64s",
   "Nora goes back across the giant's own bridge; grabs Leo; they jump into the water."
  ],
  [
   "Win + ending",
   "64-80s",
   "The hoses open up; the fire goes out. 'You came back.' 'I always come back.'"
  ]
 ],
 "draft": "NARRATOR: When the fire monster attacked, everyone in the city ran towards the canal for safety.\nNARRATOR: That's because the monster is made of fire and cannot cross water.\nNARRATOR: Nora crossed the bridge to the safe side but her younger brother Leo was left behind.\nNORA: Leo, don't move, I am coming back to get you!\nCHIEF: Nobody is allowed to cross back over, it's too dangerous.\nNARRATOR: The monster started throwing pieces of buildings into the canal to make a bridge.\nNARRATOR: Nora had an idea to use the fire hydrants to flood the plaza with water.\nNORA: Open the hydrants and flood everything!\nNARRATOR: The monster was trapped, but Leo was trapped with it.\nNARRATOR: Nora ran back across the bridge, grabbed Leo and they jumped into the water.\nNARRATOR: The firefighters sprayed the monster and it went out.\nLEO: You came back for me.\nNORA: Of course I did.",
 "critique": [
  "Hook buries the rule; open on the image (everyone running) and the reason in the second line \u2014 curiosity gap.",
  "'Cannot cross water' reads like a manual; show the foot hitting the water first, then say it in five words.",
  "Leo being left behind needs its own beat and a cut to him alone and tiny under the giant \u2014 that's the stakes shot.",
  "Nora's flaw has no setup. Add 'Nora never left anything behind' BEFORE she needs to go back, so the climax pays it off.",
  "The bridge reversal is the best idea in the story \u2014 slow it down: 'It can't cross water\u2026 but it can fill it.'",
  "Flood plan needs a rule-echo line: 'If it can't cross water \u2014 give it nothing but water.'",
  "Leo trapped on the island is a second twist; land it with a braam, not narration alone.",
  "'Of course I did' is flat. 'I always come back' turns the flaw into the theme in four words.",
  "Missing silence/air: let the jump into the water play with just a splash and steam before the next line."
 ],
